\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Recovering multiplicities from characters}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For a finite group #{G}, two finite-dimensional unitary
complex representations with equal \vocabk{characters}{fgap-001M}
are unitarily equivalent. If #{W} is irreducible and #{V} is
one of the representations, the multiplicity of #{W} is
##{\frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}\chi_V(g)\bar{\chi_W(g)}.}
Here #{\chi_V(g)=\trace(\rho_V(g))} for the action #{\rho_V}.}

\proof{
\p{On the space of complex-linear maps #{T:W\to V}, average
#{T\mapsto\rho_V(g)T\rho_W(g)^{-1}} over #{G}. Reindexing the
sum by left multiplication shows that the average takes values
in the intertwining maps. It fixes each such map, so it is a
projection onto them. Its trace equals the dimension of its
image. In orthonormal bases use the maps that take one source
basis vector to one target basis vector and kill the others.
For the summand at #{g}, the diagonal coefficient on the map
with target #{i} and source #{j} is the product of diagonal
entries #{(\rho_V(g))_{ii}(\rho_W(g)^{-1})_{jj}}. Their sum
is #{\chi_V(g)\bar{\chi_W(g)}} by unitarity.}

\p{An invariant subspace has an invariant orthogonal complement.
Splitting in finite dimension gives orthogonal sums of irreducibles.
Any nonzero intertwiner between irreducibles is invertible by
its kernel and image. An endomorphism of a complex irreducible
has an eigenvalue, and the kernel after subtracting that scalar
is a nonzero invariant subspace. The endomorphism is therefore
scalar. The dimension just calculated counts the copies of #{W},
as in the multiplicity argument of \ref{fdip-016F}.}

\p{Equal characters now give equal multiplicities for every
irreducible in either decomposition. Match summands of the same
type by invertible intertwiners, obtaining an intertwiner #{L}
of the whole spaces. The positive definite operator #{L^\dagger L}
commutes with the source representation since both actions are
unitary. Each of its eigenspaces is invariant. The operator
#{(L^\dagger L)^{-1/2}} multiplies each eigenspace by the
inverse square root of its eigenvalue. It is a finite polynomial
in the positive operator by interpolation at the distinct
eigenvalues and is also equivariant.
Hence #{L(L^\dagger L)^{-1/2}} is an isometric, onto intertwiner.}
}

\p{Mathlib's
\leanref{Representation.card_inv_mul_sum_char_mul_char_eq_finrank}
gives the character average as the dimension of the intertwiner
space when the group order is invertible in the field.
TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{Representation.nonempty_equiv_of_character_eq}
gives the linear equivalence for finite-dimensional representations
of a finite group over a characteristic-zero field. The Hermitian
metrics enter the unitary normalization above.}
